data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration needed.** Your code uses only `Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, and `Thread.Sleep` — these APIs work identically on .NET 8. It will compile and run correctly as-is.

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort): You could simplify this with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.Run`, which would give you cleaner code and better thread pool usage:

```csharp
public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
{
    Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

But again, this is purely optional — your existing code requires zero changes for the .NET 8 migration.
